WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.800
Diffusion UMMs keep three copies of every image, and learn by denoising.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.300
A discrete model needs just one copy, and every token is a prediction target.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.800
The catch: it paints one token at a time, so high resolution means long, slow sequences.

4
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.700
Compress harder and the sequence gets short, but each token must carry far more detail.

5
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.800
Meet GSTAR: group-scaled token capacity.

6
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.400
Vision encoders like Qwen-ViT already merge 2×2 sub-tokens along the channel.

7
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.500
Quantize each one before merging, and every position holds a group of codes.

8
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.700
Double the compression, quadruple the group: the bit budget stays the same.

9
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.800
Packing 2×2 turns a 32× tokenizer into an effective 64× one, losslessly.

10
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.100
A causal head is slow; a parallel head is fast, but its codes can collide.

11
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.200
Our semi-autoregressive head: one parallel pass, then a one-layer causal sweep.

12
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.200
With system optimizations, a 1024 × 1024 image takes just 2.5 s on one H200,

13
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.200
up to 21× faster than prior discrete AR models, with no loss in quality.

14
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.200
It edits images too, scoring 7.45 on GEdit-Bench.

15
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.600
GSTAR. Short sequences, rich tokens, high-quality images.
